//? Title: Training Loops: Batches, Metrics and Reproducible Resume
//? Subtitle: Separate the epoch clock, update clock and evaluation weights
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A training loop chooses rows, forms a loss, updates parameters and records progress.
Those operations use different clocks: an epoch visits the dataset, an optimizer call
advances one update, and a metric may summarize changing or fixed weights. We first
check an eleven-row example by hand, then fit a two-feature affine model to 103
observations in batches of sixteen. Finally we save a sampler midway through an epoch
and verify both its remaining rows and three future shuffles after restoration.
)
\toc

> The method


For N observations and maximum batch size B, keeping the final short batch gives
ceil(N/B) updates per epoch and visits every row once. Dropping it gives floor(N/B)
updates and omits the final N mod B rows of that epoch's order. Shuffle changes the
order, not the rows represented by the full epoch. BatchOrder starts before epoch
zero; call begin_epoch() before next(), whose empty result marks exhaustion.

If batch j has n_j observations and mean loss ell_j, its sample-weighted epoch mean is

$$
 \bar\ell=\frac{\sum_j n_j\ell_j}{\sum_j n_j}.
$$
\alttext(The epoch mean weights each batch mean by its observation count.)

During training, each ell_j uses the parameters that existed before that batch's
update. This online mean therefore combines different models. A full-data loss
evaluated after the epoch uses one fixed parameter state and can differ even when
both aggregations have the correct sample weights. Neither training loss estimates
held-out generalization. The deterministic toy fit below has no validation selection.

Sampler state contains the current permutation, cursor, epoch and shuffle RNG. Model
weights and optimizer moments/counter are separate state; save them at the same update
boundary for a complete training resume. Other stochastic layers, data transforms,
schedules and monitors may require additional state. The sampler test alone does not
establish whole-training-job resume or cross-toolchain shuffle compatibility.


The concept diagram is generated from the retained local TikZ source. LaTeX with
PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm, is needed only to regenerate it.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/training_loop_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/training_loop_concept.tikz > build/training_loop.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/training_loop_concept.svg build/training_loop_concept.dvi > build/training_loop.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/training_loop_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
