| phase_0_is_current_remainder | identical_emitted_rows |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 7 |
| 1 | 11 |
| 2 | 11 |
| 3 | 11 |


Saved cursor: 4; matched emitted rows: 40; invalid state rejected without mutation.
\image(figures/training_resume.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A sampler snapshot restores both the current permutation and the generator used by subsequent shuffles.)
\alttext(One panel plots forty emitted row indices, covering seven remaining rows and three future eleven-row epochs. Another shows zero absolute row difference after restoration. The table records counts per phase.)

> Conclusion

A reproducible training loop needs explicit row coverage, sample weighting, update
counts and evaluation timing. On this toy affine problem the coefficients approach
(1.5,-.7,.2) after 350 Adam updates, and sampler restoration reproduces forty future
row choices. These checks cover batching and ordering; whole-job resume also needs
matching model, optimizer and any other stochastic or host policy state.

> References

- Kingma, D. P. and Ba, J. (2015). [Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization|https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980]. The update rule used in the explicit minibatch fit.
- TensorFlow authors. [Writing a training loop from scratch|https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/keras/writing_a_training_loop_from_scratch]. Forward/loss/update and metric-aggregation concepts; this report uses the native eager C++ APIs.
- C++ working draft. [Shuffle|https://eel.is/c++draft/alg.random.shuffle]. Generator-controlled permutation; exact sequences need not be portable across standard-library implementations.
